I found and fixed five layout bugs. I checked them by rebuilding BokehJS and running a page in headless Chromium that recreates the clip's situations (rows, columns, a grid with a spanning chart, a legend label that grows, tick labels that change from 0–1 to 1e9). I measured each chart's outer box, point area and side borders before and after. The unit test suite passes: 2431 passed, 0 failed.

| Problem in the clip | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Tick labels cut off ("e+9") and a long legend label spilling into the next chart after the data changed | `SideLayout.has_size_changed` (`side_panel.ts`) had its comparison backwards, so the chart never re-laid itself out when an axis or legend grew | Now it fires on any size change. Growing alone wasn't enough: 4 legend unit tests failed until shrinking also triggered a relayout. |
| Charts in a row/column ("right", "left") cut off at the bottom, with the axis missing | `Row`/`Column` (`flex_box.ts`) never called `align_borders()`; only grid boxes did. A chart was moved to line up with its neighbours, but its height didn't include the extra top space. | `FlexBoxView` now calls `align_borders()` before measuring, as `CSSGridBox` does. |
| Top border not lined up, so the chart is sized too short | `BorderLayout._measure` (`border.ts`) applied the lined-up border on left, right and bottom but not on top | Added `align_border.top`. |
| Charts spanning several grid cells lined up against the wrong column/row | `align_borders()` (`alignments.ts`) used the end column for the left border and the start column for the right | Swapped to `c0` for left and `c1` for right. A spanning chart now lines up with its first column. |
| Toolbar on the left covering the y-axis labels ("tools" chart) | `plot_canvas.ts` added the left panel's elements in the opposite order from the layout (the top and inner-left panels were already reversed) | Reversed `left_els` to match. |

After the fixes:
- Charts side by side share their top and bottom borders, and stacked charts share their left and right borders.
- A chart with a fixed point area keeps that size. When its legend grows, the chart gets wider and pushes its neighbours over; when the legend shrinks, it narrows again.
- Nothing gets cut off any more.

One limit can't be fixed in layout code. If a row mixes charts with a fixed overall size and charts with a fixed point area, their heights can differ by a few pixels (247 vs 250 in my test), because both sizes are fixed by the user. Nothing is clipped in that case.

I didn't run the integration (screenshot comparison) tests. The rebuilt `build/` output isn't tracked by git, and nothing is committed.

